  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Subtropical moisture from southerly\nmonsoonal flow caused by high pressure over the four corners area,\nwill bring chances for isolated/scattered thunderstorms this\nafternoon and evening across central and northern Nevada lasting\ninto mid-week. Today is the strongest chances for thunderstorms as\nPWAT values across the area ranging above 0.8 to 1 inch of\nmoisture, with ample instability. Periods of brief moderate to\nheavy rain from thunderstorms possible with the main threat of\nthunderstorms over recently burned areas that could produce debris\nflow. Humboldt county is the exception this afternoon as the\nthunderstorms will be mainly dry due to fast storm motions that\nwill limit rainfall in the area. Tomorrow, thunderstorm chances\ncontinue in the afternoon with it more centralized over central\nNevada along US-50 corridor and extending across eastern and\nnortheastern Nevada with strongest thunderstorm chances near the\nWendover area. The greatest threat from thunderstorms tomorrow\nwill be strong gusty outflow winds up to 45-60 mph with brief\nperiods of heavy rainfall. From Monday-Wednesday, the high \npressure over the four corners will begin to shift west back \ntowards Arizona. This will limit the amount of subtropical \nmoisture entering into Nevada and drier air begins to move in. \nThere is still some pockets of ample moisture with PWATs up to \n0.7-0.85 inches shifting towards northeastern Nevada with ample \ninstability to produce additional thunderstorms in the afternoon.\nRest of the week, calmer weather conditions take hold as monsoon \nflow gets cut off with models showing better agreement that the \nhigh pressure will be centralized over Arizona and a southwesterly\nflow moves over Nevada. Temperatures remain in the 90s today and \ninto the next week with a gradual cooling trend thanks to increase\nin cloud cover, with temperatures expected to get even cooler \ndropping into the 80s by the end of the week. Winds will be light \nin the afternoons with some patches of breezy southwesterly winds \nin central Nevada and Humboldt county up to 15-20 mph, gusts 25-30\nmph through Wednesday, however thunderstorms can produce strong \ngusty erratic outflow winds up to 45-60 mph. End of the week, \nwinds will gradually become breezy thanks to a low pressure system\nover the northwest that will tighten weather gradients along with\nthe high pressure over Arizona.\n\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence for active\nthunderstorms this afternoon through mid-week due to subtropical\nmoisture from an active monsoon pattern over the southwest.\nModerate confidence of dry, calm weather conditions returning late\nweek with some breezy afternoon winds. High confidence of cooling\ntemperature trends thanks to increase in cloud coverage and cooler\nflow by the end of the week. Changes to PoP, Thunder, and Weather\ngrids were made to better reflect thunderstorm activity over the\nweekend and into Monday.",
